h to month-and-a-half and played for about eight hours. This infrequency was problematic, as many of the players needed to be reminded by Matt of elements of the storyline, how to play, etc. All the players began the campaign with the same characters they had on the stream, with the exception of Taliesin who played a dragonborn paladin for their first session. Marisha Ray and <a href=\"/critical-role/ashley-johnson-character\" class=\"entity-link\">Ashley Johnson</a> joined the game in later sessions.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        In 2014, they were approached by Felicia Day to stream their campaign live on the Geek &amp; Sundry twitch channel. When Matt told the other cast members about her offer, [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9D5RWAn2sIk the group&#039;s reactions were recorded for posterity]. In preparation for the streamed series, Matt converted the game over from Pathfinder to Dungeons &amp; Dragons, to speed up combat and because D&amp;D has more name recognition. When converting their game, Matt had to create a custom fighter&#039;s Martial Archetype for Taliesin Jaffe&#039;s character, Percy, as the Gunslinger subclass didn&#039;t exist in 5th Edition D&amp;D. On March 12, 2015 the first live episode of Critical Role was aired: .\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        The series initially aired live on the Geek &amp; Sundry [https://www.twitch.tv/geekandsundry Twitch channel] and [https://www.youtube.com/@GeekandSundry YouTube], and subsequently on the Critical Role [http://twitch.tv/criticalrole Twitch channel], on Thursdays at 7pm (Pacific Time). The first campaign and a part of the second campaign were live streamed on the Geek &amp; Sundry Twitch channel. In February 2019, Critical Role parted ways with Geek &amp; Sundry, launched their own [https://www.twitch.tv/criticalrole Twitch] and [https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCpXBGqwsBkpvcYjsJBQ7LEQ/featured YouTube] channels, and started to self-produce new shows and content under the name Critical Role Productions LLC. Eventually the live broadcasts of Critical Role and VODs aired exclusively on Critical Role&#039;s channels. Episodes are archived on the [https://critrole.com/ Critical Role website] and the [https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCpXBGqwsBkpvcYjsJBQ7LEQ Critical Role] and [https://www.youtube.com/user/geekandsundry Geek &amp; Sundry] YouTube channels.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        On 17 March 2020, Critical Role announced that it was suspending all live broadcasts of both the D&amp;D stream Critical Role and Talks Machina due to the COVID-19 pandemic. During the hiatus, they produced new content, as well as re-imagined versions of existing shows that followed the COVID-19 safety guidelines. Critical Role returned in a prerecorded format on 2 July 2020 with the broadcast of .\n    </p>\n    \n</section>","word_count":991,"uniqueness_score":"1.0000"},"entity":{"id":"a8a3b89c-b66f-421d-907d-8f8d40c4123c","name":"Critical Role","slug":"critical-role","type":"topic","kind":"topic","type_label":"Topic","short_description":"The original cast of Critical Role had been playing a Pathfinder campaign together for two years before they converted their home game into a livestreamed webseries. The campaign began as a one-off game organized for…","description":"The original cast of Critical Role had been playing a Pathfinder campaign together for two years before they converted their home game into a livestreamed webseries. The campaign began as a one-off game organized for Liam's birthday. The campaign itself started on December 8, 2012. The very first session was played by Liam O'Brien, Sam Riegel, Laura Bailey, Travis Willingham, Taliesin Jaffe, and Orion Acaba, with Matthew Mercer as the Dungeon Master. Afterwards, the group met every month to month-and-a-half and played for about eight hours. This infrequency was problematic, as many of the players needed to be reminded by Matt of elements of the storyline, how to play, etc. All the players began the campaign with the same characters they had on the stream, with the exception of Taliesin who played a dragonborn paladin for their first session. Marisha Ray and Ashley Johnson joined the game in later sessions.\n\nIn 2014, they were approached by Felicia Day to stream their campaign live on the Geek & Sundry twitch channel. When Matt told the other cast members about her offer, [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9D5RWAn2sIk the group's reactions were recorded for posterity]. In preparation for the streamed series, Matt converted the game over from Pathfinder to Dungeons & Dragons, to speed up combat and because D&D has more name recognition. When converting their game, Matt had to create a custom fighter's Martial Archetype for Taliesin Jaffe's character, Percy, as the Gunslinger subclass didn't exist in 5th Edition D&D. On March 12, 2015 the first live episode of Critical Role was aired: .\n\nThe series initially aired live on the Geek & Sundry [https://www.twitch.tv/geekandsundry Twitch channel] and [https://www.youtube.com/@GeekandSundry YouTube], and subsequently on the Critical Role [http://twitch.tv/criticalrole Twitch channel], on Thursdays at 7pm (Pacific Time). The first campaign and a part of the second campaign were live streamed on the Geek & Sundry Twitch channel. In February 2019, Critical Role parted ways with Geek & Sundry, launched their own [https://www.twitch.tv/criticalrole Twitch] and [https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCpXBGqwsBkpvcYjsJBQ7LEQ/featured YouTube] channels, and started to self-produce new shows and content under the name Critical Role Productions LLC. Eventually the live broadcasts of Critical Role and VODs aired exclusively on Critical Role's channels. Episodes are archived on the [https://critrole.com/ Critical Role website] and the [https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCpXBGqwsBkpvcYjsJBQ7LEQ Critical Role] and [https://www.youtube.com/user/geekandsundry Geek & Sundry] YouTube channels.\n\nOn 17 March 2020, Critical Role announced that it was suspending all live broadcasts of both the D&D stream Critical Role and Talks Machina due to the COVID-19 pandemic. During the hiatus, they produced new content, as well as re-imagined versions of existing shows that followed the COVID-19 safety guidelines. Critical Role returned in a prerecorded format on 2 July 2020 with the broadcast of .","evidence":"documented","universe":"critical-role","aliases":["Critical Role"],"wikipedia_url":null,"image_url":null,"image_source":null,"image_credit":null,"image_license":null,"image_usage":null,"image_alt":null},"connections":[],"rabbit_holes":[],"copyright":{"theme":"critical-role","title":"Critical Role is not owned by Ton-o-Lore.","body":"Critical Role, related names, marks, and materials are the property of Critical Role / respective rights holders. Ton-o-Lore is an independent educational encyclopedia project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by those rights holders. Identification marks, when shown, are low-resolution images used under a fair-use rationale for commentary and identification only — not free or redistributable artwork."},"subject":{"id":"critical-role","name":"Critical Role","theme":"critical-role","path":"/critical-role","logo":null,"categories":["podcasts"]}}